{"name":"Meadow Phlox","hex":"#a8afc7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"meadow phlox","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Phlox maculata, a polemoniaceous perennial flowering plant native to the eastern United States and introduced to eastern Canada."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meadow_phlox"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phlox maculata","blocks":[{"text":"Phlox maculata, commonly called meadow phlox, as well as wild sweet William and marsh phlox, is a species of flowering plant in the family Polemoniaceae, native the eastern United States and introduced to eastern Canada. It is a perennial."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Phlox_maculata_BB-1913.png/330px-Phlox_maculata_BB-1913.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phlox_maculata","wikidata":"Q15396512"},{"title":"Meadow","blocks":[{"text":"A meadow is an open habitat or field, vegetated by grasses, herbs, and other non-woody plants. Trees or shrubs may sparsely populate meadows, as long as they maintain an open character. Meadows can occur naturally under favourable conditions but are often artificially created from cleared shrub or woodland for the production of hay, fodder or livestock. Meadow habitats as a group are characterized as semi-natural grasslands, meaning that they are largely composed of species native to the region, with only limited human intervention."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Blumenwiese_bei_Obermaiselstein05.jpg/330px-Blumenwiese_bei_Obermaiselstein05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meadow","wikidata":"Q7777019"},{"title":"Phlox","blocks":[{"text":"Phlox is a genus of 68 species of perennial and annual plants in the family Polemoniaceae. They are found mostly in North America in diverse habitats from alpine tundra to open woodland and prairie. Some flower in spring, others in summer and fall. Flowers may be pale blue, violet, pink, bright red, or white. Many are fragrant."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Phlox_glaberrima.jpg/330px-Phlox_glaberrima.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phlox","wikidata":"Q652016"},{"title":"Shades of purple","blocks":[{"text":"There are numerous variations of the color purple, a sampling of which is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Shades_of_Purple.svg/330px-Shades_of_Purple.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_purple","wikidata":"Q7460344"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Phlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_%281%29.jpg/120px-Phlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Phlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_%281%29.jpg/250px-Phlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Phlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_%281%29.jpg/120px-Phlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhlox_maculata_Natascha_-_Flickr_-_peganum_(1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Phlox_maculata_NRCS-1.jpg/120px-Phlox_maculata_NRCS-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Phlox_maculata_NRCS-1.jpg/250px-Phlox_maculata_NRCS-1.jpg?utm_s